MCP AGENT OPERATING RULES

SESSION: First get_manual() or cat /man/README; then relevant page;
then help COMMAND, if needed. Avoid guessing GNU/Linux options.

MCP execute_command sends a command to sh -c, NOT a real terminal.
Use terminal_open/send/read/close for interactive PTY applications; execute_command has no interactive keyboard. Command <=119 ASCII bytes,
output <=4096 bytes, nominal child execution timeout 3 seconds.
Calls create extra processes; PicoNIX has only six 24 KiB user slots.
Use ps before complex operations. Never kill mcpd accidentally.

DO NOT RUN via execute_command: full-screen or interactive mem,
mb, watch, edit, vi, fd, fbed, gpio, pong, interactive python,
trace follow, or indefinite foreground servers. Use finite commands
instead: ps, hexdump, mem -s, trace show, cat, ip, pwmctl status.
Trace run may spawn more processes and consume much output: prefer
trace on -s, execute a short command, trace show, then trace on.

READ-ONLY -> MUTATE -> VERIFY:
  1. Identify path, pin, device ownership and output size.
  2. Read current value; avoid unbounded cat of streaming devices.
  3. Explain risk, request authorization for hardware or destructive
     file changes, and issue one short modifying command.
  4. Verify with another short read-only command.
  5. Report observed result; don't claim success from tool invocation.

write_file creates/overwrites a /tmp/NAME ASCII file, max 2048 B.
Use distinct names to avoid destroying work. C cc input <=1024 B.
The assembler writes /tmp/a.out; do not run a stale binary after
an as failure. Ensure successful compiler/assembler outputs first.

mcpd & exposes all eight MCP tools, including get_manual,
execute_command, write_file and terminal_*. The service has NO
authentication or TLS. Use only an isolated trusted LAN.

On 'incomplete chunked read', connection closed before HTTP response
completed. Check mcpd is alive with ps from a physical terminal,
check process slots, and retry read-only operations cautiously.
A modifying command might have executed before the response failed.
Use bounded hexdump and trace; don't fill TCP with endless output.

Before changing hardware, read gpio-board and check pins. Pico W
GP25 is NOT the board LED. Report command success separately from
physical observation. Never remove files not created for this task.
Read shell-syntax/compatibility before issuing composite commands.

For interactive vi/fd sessions, read get_manual(topic="vi") or
get_manual(topic="fd") BEFORE sending keystrokes. This is NOT
full Vim and fd is NOT a Linux fd search CLI. Read screen after
operations and verify file changes using a separate read-only tool.
